# vtium's unit starts vtium again after a failure (ADR-1401 D9,
# docs/systemd.md). Under the open policy, which runs with no head file,
# the getty fallback takes the VT at the first failure; under a closed
# policy vtium comes back on its VT, and past systemd's start limit of 5
# starts in 10 seconds the VT is left with no login. The steps describe
# systemd 254 and later.
#
# The checks kill vtium, so this guide runs in a desktop terminal, never
# as vtium's session. It acts on one spare VT, N, that is not on screen
# and has no login on it, and it needs vtium installed as the unit of that
# VT: kmsconvt@.service and kmsconvt-getty@.service from make install-tree,
# with Restart=on-failure, and /usr/bin/vtium. From the checkout's top
# directory, with the desktop on VT D:
#
#   GUIDE_VT=N GUIDE_DESKTOP=D tools/vt/checks/guide.sh tools/vt/checks/restart.guide
#
# Each kill goes through systemctl to the one unit of VT N, so it reaches
# that vtium alone; pkill -x vtium would kill every vtium on the machine.

title: vtium starts again after a failure

intro: This guide acts on VT {vt} alone, from this desktop terminal. sudo
intro: asks for your password here. Steps 6 to 9 check the closed policy:
intro: skip all four (s, then Enter) to leave /etc/vtium untouched.

step: VT {vt} is a spare VT, and this terminal is on the desktop
run: echo "inside vtium: ${VTIUM_CONSOLE:+yes}${VTIUM_CONSOLE:-no}"; echo "on screen: $(cat /sys/class/tty/tty0/active)"; echo "logins on tty{vt}: $(loginctl list-sessions --no-legend | grep -cw 'tty{vt}')"; systemctl --version | head -n 1
expect: "inside vtium: no", "on screen: tty{desktop}", "logins on tty{vt}:
expect: 0", and systemd 254 or later. If any of these differs, answer n,
expect: then press q at the next step to end the checks.

step: The unit of VT {vt} is vtium's
run: systemctl show -p ExecStart -p Restart -p OnFailure kmsconvt@tty{vt}.service
expect: ExecStart runs /usr/bin/vtium with --vt=tty{vt} --no-switchvt,
expect: Restart=on-failure, and OnFailure=kmsconvt-getty@tty{vt}.service.
expect: If ExecStart runs kmscon, or Restart is no, vtium's unit is not
expect: installed: answer n, then press q at the next step.

step: vtium starts on VT {vt}
run: sudo systemctl start kmsconvt@tty{vt}.service && sleep 1 && systemctl show -p Id -p ActiveState kmsconvt@tty{vt}.service getty@tty{vt}.service
do: press Ctrl-Alt-F{vt}, look, then come back with Ctrl-Alt-F{desktop}.
expect: kmsconvt@tty{vt} active and getty@tty{vt} inactive, since vtium
expect: replaces the getty. VT {vt} shows vtium's login prompt, drawn whole.

step: One kill under the open policy hands VT {vt} to a getty
run: sudo systemctl kill --kill-whom=main --signal=SIGKILL kmsconvt@tty{vt}.service; sleep 3; systemctl show -p Id -p ActiveState kmsconvt@tty{vt}.service getty@tty{vt}.service
do: then press Ctrl-Alt-F{vt}, look, and come back with Ctrl-Alt-F{desktop}.
expect: kmsconvt@tty{vt} inactive or failed, and getty@tty{vt} active.
expect: With no head file the policy is open, so the getty fallback starts
expect: at the first failure and stops vtium, which systemd had started
expect: again. VT {vt} shows the kernel console's login prompt, not vtium.

step: vtium takes VT {vt} back
run: sudo systemctl start kmsconvt@tty{vt}.service && sleep 1 && systemctl show -p Id -p ActiveState kmsconvt@tty{vt}.service getty@tty{vt}.service
expect: kmsconvt@tty{vt} active, and getty@tty{vt} inactive again.

step: vtium reads a closed policy
run: [ ! -e /etc/vtium/heads.conf ] && /usr/bin/vtium check-config tools/vt/checks/restart-closed.conf && sudo install -D -m 0644 -o root -g root tools/vt/checks/restart-closed.conf /etc/vtium/heads.conf && sudo systemctl restart kmsconvt@tty{vt}.service && sleep 1 && ls -l /run/vtium/tty{vt}.closed
do: skip this step and the three after it (s, then Enter) to leave
do: /etc/vtium untouched. The command stops at once if a head file is
do: already installed, so that file is never replaced.
expect: check-config finds the file valid, and /run/vtium/tty{vt}.closed is
expect: listed: vtium read policy = closed and wrote the marker that keeps
expect: the getty away. The head is not isolated, so the keyboards still
expect: type on VT {vt} and Ctrl-Alt-F{desktop} still switches back.

step: One kill under the closed policy brings vtium back
run: sudo systemctl kill --kill-whom=main --signal=SIGKILL kmsconvt@tty{vt}.service; sleep 3; systemctl show -p Id -p ActiveState -p SubState kmsconvt@tty{vt}.service getty@tty{vt}.service
do: then press Ctrl-Alt-F{vt}, look, and come back with Ctrl-Alt-F{desktop}.
expect: kmsconvt@tty{vt} active and running, and getty@tty{vt} inactive:
expect: systemd started vtium again after 100 ms, and the marker kept the
expect: getty away. VT {vt} shows vtium's login prompt, started afresh.

step: Past the start limit, VT {vt} is left with no login
run: for i in 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8; do sudo systemctl kill --kill-whom=main --signal=SIGKILL kmsconvt@tty{vt}.service 2>/dev/null; sleep 0.5; done; sleep 2; systemctl show -p Id -p ActiveState -p Result kmsconvt@tty{vt}.service getty@tty{vt}.service; ls -l /run/vtium/tty{vt}.closed
do: stay on the desktop: nothing runs on VT {vt} now.
expect: kmsconvt@tty{vt} failed with Result=start-limit-hit, since systemd
expect: allows 5 starts in 10 seconds and refused the next one;
expect: getty@tty{vt} inactive; and the marker still listed.

step: Recovery after the start limit
run: sudo cmp -s tools/vt/checks/restart-closed.conf /etc/vtium/heads.conf && sudo rm /etc/vtium/heads.conf; sudo rmdir /etc/vtium 2>/dev/null; sudo systemctl reset-failed kmsconvt@tty{vt}.service; sudo systemctl start kmsconvt@tty{vt}.service; sleep 1; systemctl show -p Id -p ActiveState kmsconvt@tty{vt}.service; test -e /run/vtium/tty{vt}.closed && echo "the marker is still there" || echo "no marker"
expect: kmsconvt@tty{vt} active again, and "no marker": with the head file
expect: gone vtium runs the open policy and removes the marker. The
expect: command removes the head file only if it is this guide's own.

step: VT {vt} gets its getty back
run: sudo systemctl start getty@tty{vt}.service && sleep 1 && systemctl show -p Id -p ActiveState kmsconvt@tty{vt}.service getty@tty{vt}.service
expect: kmsconvt@tty{vt} inactive and getty@tty{vt} active, as VT {vt} was
expect: before this guide.
